          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A swelling becomes less mobile when the patient tenses the underlying muscle. Wh</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A swelling becomes less mobile when the patient tenses the underlying muscle. Which anatomical plane does this swelling most likely occupy?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Within the skin (dermis)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A dermal swelling is tethered to the skin itself and moves with it. Its mobility is not specifically affected by muscle contraction beneath it.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>In the subcutaneous fat</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A subcutaneous swelling lies superficial to the deep fascia and remains freely mobile when the muscle beneath contracts, because the fascia separates the two layers.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Deep to or within the muscle</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. When muscle contraction restricts a swelling's mobility, the swelling lies deep to or within that muscle layer. This is one of two key bedside tests for determining the anatomical plane.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>On the periosteum (bone surface)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A periosteal swelling would be bony-hard and immobile at all times, regardless of muscle contraction. The muscle-tensing test distinguishes superficial from deep-to-muscle lesions.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Two bedside manoeuvres determine the plane: if the overlying skin can be pinched freely, the swelling is subcutaneous; if it becomes less mobile on muscle tensing, it lies deep to or within the muscle."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The muscle-tensing test and the skin-pinch test together determine the anatomical plane of any swelling — a critical first step in structured examination."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
