          },
          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with a warm, red, tender area on the leg. The margins are ill</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with a warm, red, tender area on the leg. The margins are ill-defined and there is no palpable fluid collection. Which organism most likely explains this pattern of infection?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Staphylococcus aureus</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Staphylococcus aureus characteristically localises infection and walls off pus to form abscesses. It does not typically produce the diffuse, spreading pattern seen here.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Streptococcus pyogenes</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Streptococcus pyogenes spreads through tissue planes without forming a collection. The ill-defined margins and absence of pus are hallmarks of streptococcal cellulitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Escherichia coli</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>E. coli is associated with urinary tract and intra-abdominal infections, not with superficial soft-tissue cellulitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Clostridium perfringens</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Clostridium perfringens causes gas gangrene with crepitus and extensive tissue necrosis — a far more severe and rapidly progressive presentation than cellulitis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the organism–pattern distinction: Staph localises pus; Strep spreads through planes. An ill-defined spreading infection without a collection points to Streptococcus pyogenes."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The key organism–pattern link: Streptococcus pyogenes spreads through tissue planes (cellulitis), while Staphylococcus aureus localises and walls off pus (abscesses)."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
