          "label": "<p>Which single measurement is the most important prognostic factor for malignant m</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which single measurement is the most important prognostic factor for malignant melanoma?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Breslow thickness</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Breslow thickness measures the vertical depth of tumour invasion in millimetres. It determines both prognosis and the width of the excision margin for definitive surgery.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Maximum horizontal diameter of the lesion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Horizontal diameter helps describe the lesion but correlates poorly with prognosis. It is the vertical depth of invasion, not the surface spread, that determines outcome.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Number of colours within the lesion</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Colour variation is the 'C' in the ABCDE diagnostic rule and raises suspicion for melanoma, but it is a diagnostic feature, not the measurement used for staging and prognosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Distance from the nearest lymph node basin</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Proximity to lymph nodes does not determine melanoma prognosis. Breslow thickness drives staging decisions, including whether sentinel lymph node biopsy is indicated.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Breslow thickness — the vertical depth of tumour invasion in millimetres — is the single most important prognostic factor for melanoma. It is measured from a full-thickness excision biopsy specimen."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Breslow thickness is obtained by full-thickness excision biopsy — never by shave biopsy, which would destroy the measurement. It guides both prognosis and surgical margins."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
