          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Patient A has a warm, diffusely tender forearm with ill-defined redness and no f</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Patient A has a warm, diffusely tender forearm with ill-defined redness and no fluctuance. Patient B has a tender, fluctuant forearm swelling with surrounding erythema, fever, and tachycardia. Applying the three bedside questions for inflamed lesions, which analysis is correct?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Both patients require incision and drainage as the primary intervention</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Patient A has no fluctuance and therefore no pus collection to drain. Incision and drainage is indicated only when fluctuance confirms a walled-off collection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Both patients can be managed with oral antibiotics and outpatient review</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Patient B has fluctuance (pus requiring drainage) and signs of systemic illness (fever, tachycardia). Oral antibiotics alone would be inadequate and potentially dangerous.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Patient A needs antibiotics and elevation; Patient B needs drainage, intravenous antibiotics, and sepsis management</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Applying the three questions separately: Patient A — no pus, not spreading aggressively, not septic → antibiotics and elevation. Patient B — pus present (fluctuance), spreading (erythema), systemically unwell (fever, tachycardia) → drain, IV antibiotics, and manage sepsis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Patient A has a deeper infection because the erythema is more diffuse</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Diffuse erythema without a collection indicates spreading superficial infection (cellulitis), not a deeper process. Patient B's fluctuant collection actually involves a deeper, walled-off component.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Apply the three bedside questions independently to each patient: (1) Is there pus? (2) Is it spreading? (3) Is the patient systemically unwell? The combination of answers determines the management pathway."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The three bedside questions — Is there pus? Is it spreading? Is the patient systemically unwell? — yield different answers for each patient and lead to clearly different management pathways."
                }
              ],
